          "id": 3002,
          "title": "🌍 How Geofencing Works Here",
          "description": "Explains the geofencing_countries default, how to tune it live, and the #1 reason this map can appear empty even when it shouldn't.",
          "links": [],
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "transformations": [],
              "queryOptions": {}
            }
          },
          "vizConfig": {
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "group": "text",
            "version": "",
            "spec": {
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**How geofencing works.** `geofencing.geofencing_countries` in `config.yaml` currently defaults to blocking `RU`, `KP`, `IR` — any connection whose GeoIP-resolved country matches this list gets an instant CRITICAL verdict, same severity as a confirmed malicious IOC. This is **blocklist-only** (there's no allowlist mode) and it's a `[LIVE]` setting — edit the list in `config.yaml` and it takes effect within seconds, no restart.\n\n**Why the map might look empty:** geofencing (and this whole map) depends entirely on `paths.geoip_db` loading successfully at startup. If it fails to load, the engine logs one line and silently disables GeoIP features — geofencing will never fire and this map will show nothing, even with real traffic. Check your startup logs first before assuming your network is quiet.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              },
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              }
            }
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-3001": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "id": 3001,
          "title": "🗺️ Top Risky Countries (GeoIP)",
          "description": "The same home_ids_geo_risk data as the map above, as a sortable table — useful when you want exact numbers rather than reading map marker sizes. topk(10, ...) shows your 10 highest-risk countries by connection volume/severity.",
          "links": [],
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "query": {
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "group": "prometheus",
                      "version": "v0",
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "${PROMETHEUS_DS}"
                      },
                      "spec": {
                        "expr": "topk(10, home_ids_geo_risk)",
                        "format": "table",
                        "instant": true
                      }
                    },
                    "refId": "A",
                    "hidden": false
                  }
                }
              ],
              "transformations": [],
              "queryOptions": {}
